          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p In practice, functors between additive categories are generally assumed to be additive.
          #SufficientConditions.num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Each of the following conditions is sufficient for guaranteeing that a functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{B}
              preserves biproducts (where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
              are categories with a zero object):
            %ul
              %li
                The functor preserves finite products (for instance, because it’s a right adjoint) and any product in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
                is a biproduct.
              %li
                The functor preserves finite coproducts (for instance, because it’s a left adjoint) and any coproduct in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
                is a biproduct.
              %li The functor preserves finite products and coproducts.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
          #HomFunctor.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mi op
                    %mo ×
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(-,-) \colon \mathcal{A}^{op}\times \mathcal{A} \to Ab
              is additive in both arguments separately (using the nature of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproducts"} biproducts
              and that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functors+preserve+limits"} hom-functors preserve limits
              in each variable separately).
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo =
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A} = R
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \in \mathcal{A}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+modules"} tensor product of modules
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)\otimes N \colon \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{A}
          %p
            In fact these examples are generic, see prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RightExactAdditiveIsTensor"}
            below.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+abelian+group"} solid abelian group
              is by definition an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+functor"} additive functor
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_enriched_functors
            Relation to
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+category"} additive category
            canonically carries the structure of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab-enriched+category"} Ab-enriched category
            where the
            = succeed "-enrichment" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproduct"} biproduct
          #AdditiveIsAbEnriched.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              With respect to the canonical
              = succeed "-structure" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab-enriched+category"} Ab-enriched category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{B}
              are equivalently
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+functors"} enriched functors
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              An
              = succeed "-enriched" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ab
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab-enriched+category"} Ab-enriched categories
              are
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+complete+category"} Cauchy colimits
          %h3#CharacterizationByExactness Characterization of right exact additive functors
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R, R'
            be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
          %p
            The following is the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Watts+theorem"} Eilenberg-Watts theorem
          #RightExactAdditiveIsTensor.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              If an additive functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi R
